From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 09:00:51 -0643
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: exynos: re-apply the UFS pin state when resuming
 with the link down

On the Pixel 9a (zumapro) the UFS pads, gph5-0 (the device's reference
clock) and gph5-1 (its reset), came back from a system suspend as plain
inputs (CON 0) on about one wake in a hundred. The device then gets no
clock and no reset, the host reports no device present through every
link startup (HCS 0x8, no DP), and the resume fails with the root
filesystem read-only until a reboot reprograms the pads. No host-side
retry reaches a device whose pads are disconnected: a host reset, a
longer reset pulse, a PHY power cycle, waits of up to 1.6 s between
tries and a real VCC power cycle were each tried and each failed the
same way.

pinctrl-samsung's own save and restore of the bank is not enough here:
its restore wrote the pads' CON back as 0x22 at every wake, and on the
failing wakes the pads still read as inputs afterwards, so whatever
takes their function away does it after that restore.

Select the UFS node's pin state again in the resume callback whenever
the link is down (hibern8 included, so at rpm_lvl 3 every runtime
resume does it too), once the host's clocks are on. The node needs a
"sleep" pin state for this, selected in the suspend callback, because
pinctrl_select_state() is a no-op for an unchanged state; the zumapro
device tree gets it in the next commit.

With this change 300 RTC-woken s2idle cycles completed on a Pixel 9a,
on the integration tree with the cores kept out of C2 for the sleep,
with no storage failure (2026-11-07/08); before it one wake in 15 to
215 lost the storage.

Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c
index 4eadca2fe..49d0ce212 100644
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/phy/phy.h>
+#include <linux/pinctrl/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 
@@ -1886,8 +1887,10 @@ static int exynos_ufs_suspend(struct ufs_hba *hba, enum ufs_pm_op pm_op,
 	if (ufs->drv_data->suspend)
 		ufs->drv_data->suspend(ufs);
 
-	if (!ufshcd_is_link_active(hba))
+	if (!ufshcd_is_link_active(hba)) {
 		phy_power_off(ufs->phy);
+		pinctrl_pm_select_sleep_state(hba->dev);
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -1896,8 +1899,19 @@ static int exynos_ufs_resume(struct ufs_hba *hba, enum ufs_pm_op pm_op)
 {
 	struct exynos_ufs *ufs = ufshcd_get_variant(hba);
 
-	if (!ufshcd_is_link_active(hba))
+	if (!ufshcd_is_link_active(hba)) {
+		/*
+		 * On zumapro the UFS pads (the device's reference clock and
+		 * reset) came back from a system suspend as plain inputs on
+		 * about one wake in a hundred, and the host then saw no device
+		 * however often it reset it. Select the pin state again now
+		 * that the link is down and the host's clocks are on (at
+		 * rpm_lvl 3 this runs on every runtime resume too); the
+		 * "sleep" state exists so the default is really written again.
+		 */
+		pinctrl_pm_select_default_state(hba->dev);
 		phy_power_on(ufs->phy);
+	}
 
 	exynos_ufs_config_smu(ufs);
 	exynos_ufs_fmp_resume(hba);
